      "side": 1,
      "message": "The problem is crgb_data[2] + crgb_data[1] can be greater than 1 \u003c\u003c 16, but I assume crgb_data[2] and crgb_data[1] are each less than 1 \u003c\u003c 16.\n\nIn that case, we can do n_interval \u003d FP_DIV(INT_TO_FP(crgb_data[2]), INT_TO_FP(crgb_data[0])) + FP_DIV(INT_TO_FP(crgb_data[1]), INT_TO_FP(crgb_data[0]))\n\nGiven we save 15 bits after the decimal point, dividing before addition is not as bad with fp_t as it is with integer.\nSee if it can help below.",
      "revId": "5f31e97dc1b3609851d44397425b020b0035f81a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a7d47acf_34a0ad8d",
        "filename": "board/dooly/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 206,
      "author": {
        "id": 1383392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-13T09:35:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The elements of crgb_data are unsigned 16-bit integers, so storing them as signed integers requires at least 17 bits to avoid overflow. However the fixed point implementation only allocates 16 bits to the integer part, so any occurrence of INT_TO_FP(crgb_data[0..3])) is unsafe, e.g.\n\u003e INT_TO_FP(65535) \u003d INT_TO_FP(0xFFFF) \u003d 0xFFFF0000 \u003d -65536.\n\nI\u0027ve switched the code over to 12-bit fixed precision in the latest patchset which avoids this problem.",
      "parentUuid": "6d83614d_3c1d299a",
      "revId": "5f31e97dc1b3609851d44397425b020b0035f81a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
